[Verse 42]

* Srimad Bhagavad Gita *

Many types of beings exist. Therefore, it is said in the Gita. And dogs, donkeys, pigs, etc. are considered lowly. Similarly, there is no object in the three worlds that is considered superior and no object that is considered lowly. The crow, the vulture, etc. All these have favorable and unfavorable circumstances, but they are not the same. Now, the beings other than humans, such as animals and birds, have these circumstances. The meaning is that upward, middle, and downward movements are also considered high and low; just as cows, etc. are considered superior, there are also various types of caste and circumstances in them. Parishishta Bhaav — In the fourth chapter, the Lord said that I have created the four varnas by dividing them into good qualities and actions—'gunakarmavibhagashah' (4.13), and here it is said that the actions of the four varnas have been divided into three qualities by their inherent nature—'svabhavaprabhavair gunaih'. In the fourth chapter, the birth of the four varnas is mentioned, and here the actions of the four varnas are mentioned. The meaning is that in the fourth chapter, the Lord explained that the birth of the four varnas occurred according to the good qualities and actions of the previous birth, and here it is explained that the actions of the four varnas should be such that their future progress will be according to those actions. Samabandh — Now, the Lord explains the natural actions of the Brahmins. Shamo damastapah shoucham kshantirarjavamev cha. Jnanam vijnanam astitkyam brahmkarm svabhavajam 42. Shamo = controlling the mind;
Kshama = forgiveness;
- controlling the senses;
Arjavam = honesty;
= in the body, mind, etc. Damah = self-control;
= for the sake of following dharma;
Kshat = tolerating difficulties;
Jnanam = simplicity;
Astitkyam = faith in the Supreme, Vedas, etc.;
= (all these are)
Tapah = knowledge of the Vedas, etc.;
= (all these are)
Shoucham = purity of the body, mind, etc.;
= (all these are)
Brahmakarm, svabhavajam = these are the natural actions of the Brahmins. Explanation: 'Shamo' - control the mind wherever you want to, and keep it pure, and wherever you want to remove it, remove it - this is called 'shama' or controlling the mind. 'Damah' - the sense that you want to use, use it, and the sense that you want to remove, remove it - this is called 'dam' or controlling the senses. 'Tapah' - in the Gita, the description of the tapas of the body, speech, and mind is given (17th chapter, 14th, 15th, and 16th shlokas), but here, in reality, 'tap' means tolerating difficulties or hardships with a happy heart, i.e., tolerating difficulties or hardships with a happy heart. 'Shoucham' - keeping the mind, intellect, senses, body, etc. pure - this is called 'shouch' or the practice of purity. 'Kshantih' - even if someone insults, abuses, or causes pain, and even if you have the power to punish them, do not punish them, but forgive them with a happy heart - this is called 'kshanti' or forgiveness. 'Arjavam' - having simplicity in behavior and speech, and not having deceit, cunning, or hypocrisy in the mind - this is called 'arjav' or honesty. 'Jnanam' - studying the Vedas, Shastras, Puranas, etc. properly, and having a proper understanding of their meaning, and having knowledge of duty and non-duty - this is called 'jnan' or knowledge. 'Vijnanam' - knowing the objects of the yajna, such as the sruck, srava, etc. - this is called 'vijnan' or knowledge.

